<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portuguese Malacca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1511">1511</a>–<a href="/years/1641">1641</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> was the Portuguese colony at Malacca on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, founded when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> captured the city from its sultan in 1511. Its fortress, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">A Famosa</a>, controlled traffic through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and served as a base for Portuguese trade and diplomacy across the Indonesian archipelago. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> took it after a long siege in 1641.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca.jpg" alt="Portuguese Malacca" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself, based on ancient national symbol. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(1640).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I of Portugal</a> sent Diogo Lopes de Sequeira to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1509 to make a trade agreement. Sultan Mahmud Shah at first received him well, but Muslim merchants at court persuaded him that the Portuguese were a threat, and he attacked their four ships, capturing and torturing several men. In April 1511 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> with around 1,200 men. When the sultan refused him leave to build a fortified trading post, the city fell on 24 August after 40 days of fighting, and a fortress was raised on the site of the mosque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The exiled sultan, aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pati-unus">Pati Unus</a> of Demak, tried to retake the city, and raids from his new base at Bintan caused severe hardship until the Portuguese razed it in 1526. His sons founded the sultanates of Perak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>. In 1550 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratu-kalinyamat">Ratu Kalinyamat</a>, queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a>, sent 4,000 soldiers to help Johor; more than 2,000 of them were killed. Attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> failed in 1568 and, with Jepara, in 1574, and in 1629 Portuguese sources claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> of Aceh lost all his ships and some 19,000 men.</p><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, Jorge Álvares reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1513 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a> led an embassy that ended in imprisonment, though by 1557 the Ming allowed the Portuguese to settle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>. As traders turned to rival ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, Malacca declined. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> besieged it in 1606 and attacked again in 1616, before taking it with its local allies in January 1641.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> answered to the governor or viceroy of Portuguese India at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> and was run by a captain-major in the fortress. In 1552 it received a city charter and a senate speaking for the married settlers, the casados, who often married local women. The pre-conquest offices of bendahara, temenggung and shahbandar were kept and filled from among non-Muslims. A bishopric under the Archbishopric of Goa served the town, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priests such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> passed through on their way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Outside the walls lay the suburbs of Tranqueira, Yler and Sabba; a community descended from Portuguese settlers still lives at Ujong Pasir.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portuguese Malacca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within a generation Aceh had become the leading Muslim power of northern Sumatra and the chief rival of <b>Portuguese Malacca</b> for the trade of the strait, drawing Muslim shipping that had once called at Malacca to its own harbour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of <b>Portuguese Malacca</b> in 1641, Johor's ports flourished as centres of the trade between India, China and the archipelago, and in the late seventeenth century Riau was among the busiest Malay entrepôts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Portuguese Malacca</b> never controlled the trade it had seized; short of money and men, it watched Asian traders bypass it for Johor and other ports, and the single policed emporium of the strait gave way to scattered rivals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1522 Sunda made a treaty with the <b>Portuguese of Malacca</b>, allowing them to build a fort at Sunda Kalapa in return for help, but before the Portuguese returned, forces from Demak and Cirebon under Fatahillah captured Banten and, in 1527, Sunda Kalapa itself, defeating a Portuguese landing…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
